Destructuring and parallel assignment
=====================================

Sysl supports tuple destructure on declaration and parallel
assignment between existing variables, in three shapes
(reference §"Destructuring and Parallel Assignment"):

  - `val q, r = divmod(17, 5)` — declares q, r from a tuple
    return; immutable.
  - `q, r = divmod(17, 5)` — bare form, declares as `var` when
    both names are new.
  - `a, b = b, a` — parallel assignment to two existing
    `var`s; RHS evaluated fully before either store.
  - `x, y = p` — destructure a named struct by field order.

This file pins each shape across all seven backends.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

A tuple-returning helper: divmod. Use a file-local prefix
(`Da` for "destructure-assign") on the fn name to avoid the
module-flat namespace pinch.

    da_divmod(a: int, b: int) -> (int, int) = a / b, a % b

A two-field point struct used for struct destructure.

    struct DaPoint
        x: int
        y: int

`val` destructure from a tuple return
--------------------------------------

The simplest case: two `val` bindings receive the two-tuple
result of a function call.

    #test
    test_val_destructure_tuple() -> unit
        val q, r = da_divmod(17, 5)
        assert(q == 3, s"q should be 3, got $q")
        assert(r == 2, s"r should be 2, got $r")

`var` destructure from a tuple return
-------------------------------------

`var q, r = ...` declares mutable bindings. The two results
must be independently mutable afterwards.

    #test
    test_var_destructure_tuple_then_mutate() -> unit
        var q, r = da_divmod(20, 6)
        assert(q == 3, "q before mutate")
        assert(r == 2, "r before mutate")
        q = q + 100
        r = r + 200
        assert(q == 103, s"q after mutate, got $q")
        assert(r == 202, s"r after mutate, got $r")

Parenthesized form
------------------

`(q, r) = ...` is equivalent to the bare form. Pin that the
parens don't change semantics or binding.

    #test
    test_parenthesized_destructure() -> unit
        val (q, r) = da_divmod(100, 7)
        assert(q == 14, s"q should be 14, got $q")
        assert(r == 2, s"r should be 2, got $r")

Parallel assignment — swap
--------------------------

`a, b = b, a` with two existing `var`s. The RHS is fully
evaluated before either store, so the swap is atomic from the
two-variable perspective.

    #test
    test_parallel_assign_swap() -> unit
        var a = 10
        var b = 20
        a, b = b, a
        assert(a == 20, s"a should be 20 after swap, got $a")
        assert(b == 10, s"b should be 10 after swap, got $b")

Parallel assignment — three-way rotation
----------------------------------------

`a, b, c = c, a, b` rotates three values. RHS evaluated fully
before any store, so the rotation is atomic.

    #test
    test_parallel_assign_rotate() -> unit
        var a = 1
        var b = 2
        var c = 3
        a, b, c = c, a, b
        assert(a == 3, s"a should be 3, got $a")
        assert(b == 1, s"b should be 1, got $b")
        assert(c == 2, s"c should be 2, got $c")

Struct destructure by field order
---------------------------------

`x, y = p` extracts the two fields of a named struct in
declaration order — `x` gets `p.x`, `y` gets `p.y`.

    #test
    test_struct_destructure_val() -> unit
        val p = DaPoint(11, 22)
        val x, y = p
        assert(x == 11, s"x should be 11 (p.x), got $x")
        assert(y == 22, s"y should be 22 (p.y), got $y")

Destructure does not mutate source
----------------------------------

After `val x, y = p`, the source struct `p` still holds its
original field values. The destructure copies, it doesn't
move/consume.

    #test
    test_destructure_does_not_consume_source() -> unit
        val p = DaPoint(7, 8)
        val x, y = p
        assert(x == 7 && y == 8, "destructure values")
        assert(p.x == 7 && p.y == 8, "source still intact")
